WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.040
Perovskite is one of the most common crystal
structures found on the planet.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.279
But common doesn’t mean boring.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.140
While it was originally discovered as a mineral,

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.460
this amazing crystal shape shows up everywhere,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.940
and is a common subject,

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.240
for cutting-edge scientific research.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.720
For starters, Earth scientists are fascinated with it,

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.420
because even though it's the most abundant kind of mineral on the planet,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.880
We hardly ever see it.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.080
Most of it is buried deep within the earth’s mantle.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.740
Perovskites make up 93 percent of the lower
mantle’s mass,

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:37.660
and 38 percent of the total mass of the planet.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:40.060
There are several different kinds of perovskite,

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.860
but they share a similar, dense crystalline structure.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.800
In the mantle, silicate-perovskites are the
most common;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.240
the high density of these minerals

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:54.140
create the sharp boundary at a depth of 660 kilometers

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.960
that separates the less dense upper mantle,

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.320
from the more dense, perovskite-rich lower mantle.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.040
Below 2600 kilometers,

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.000
the pressure crushes, silicate-perovskites into

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.340
a new mineral called post-perovskite.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:14.140
Post-perovskite is mechanically weaker, but
has a higher electrical conductivity

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:18.660
that  allows pockets of the mineral to interact
with Earth’s magnetic field.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.980
Researchers theorize that all terrestrial
planets bigger than Mars,

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.000
have a similar abundance of perovskites deep beneath their surface,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.740
so mapping out these mineral transitions allows us

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:34.220
to not only learn more about the internal structure of our planet,

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.200
but other planets as well.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:40.940
Perovskite’s knack for conductivity brings
to the fore another quirk

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.259
that makes it the perfect target for research.

00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:49.899
Metal-halide perovskites are a type of crystal
that can act as a semiconductor.

00:01:49.899 --> 00:01:55.000
So scientists are looking at how it can be
incorporated into various technologies.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.360
In 2009, researchers discovered that by shining
light on perovskite,

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.240
they were able to induce a small electric charge,

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.920
creating a minuscule solar cell.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:13.840
Since then, the efficiency of perovskite-based
solar cells has increased to over 22 percent.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:19.180
These panels generate electricity via a thin
layer of Lead halide perovskite film

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.740
on top of a coating of conductive oxide.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:28.760
One of the benefits to using perovskites in
solar cells is their “defect-tolerance.”

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.920
Unlike silicon cells, where everything has
to be aligned perfectly to work,

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.480
and any damage renders the entire device unusable,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.790
perovskite cells can be simply layered onto a surface

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:42.520
and generate an electric charge,

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:46.120
making manufacturing easier and cheaper.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.360
But while engineers are hopeful that perovskite
could help to bring down the price of solar energy,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.520
they have some downsides for now.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:58.160
The perovskite cells are small, fragile, and
degrade much faster

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.840
than conventional silicon crystal panels:

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:05.400
on the order of hours and days, instead of years.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.840
If perovskite can take in light, could it
also make it?

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.240
Researchers are looking into improving screens and LEDs,

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:19.300
Using lead halide perovskites as “quantum dot” nanocrystals

00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:25.599
On a LCD TV screen, pixels are made up of
3 colors: red, green, and blue.

00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:33.530
Tests with prototype perovskite pixels used
a single kind of light source, blue LEDs,

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:37.200
with perovskite/polymer beads on top to control
the color.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.099
When the blue light shines through the beads,
the color of the light that makes it through

00:03:42.099 --> 00:03:45.400
comes out in a green or red hue.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:51.530
While silicon quantum dot displays are out
on the market already, they are very expensive.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:55.140
So like solar panels, using halide perovskites

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.900
could bring down the cost of manufacturing immensely.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.240
The colors created by this perovskite prototype,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.680
exceeded some television and computer monitor standards

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.970
good enough for use in a future TV.

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:13.780
Even though each of these uses has some roadblocks
to adoption,

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:18.080
the massive increase in research and studies about perovskite

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:21.100
means we’ll be seeing a lot of this crystal structure

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.200
in the years to come.

